# Batch 10 (2026-10-22): North and Sub-Saharan Africa, Southern Africa, Mesoamerica, Native North America, Eastern Europe and the Slavic world, the medieval Islamic world, Australia, 19th-century anomalies and the history of science
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[
# --- part 1: Africa (North, Sub-Saharan and Southern)
X('jebel-sahaba','Jebel Sahaba: the Nile cemetery of the oldest known war',H,'confirmed',2,-11500,['archaeological','skeletal analysis','radiocarbon'],
 "Jebel Sahaba (Site 117) is a Late Pleistocene cemetery on the Nile near the Sudan–Egypt border, found in 1964 by a team led by Fred Wendorf during the rescue campaigns before Lake Nasser flooded the valley; the site now lies under the lake. It held the remains of 61 people of both sexes and all ages. A 2021 re-examination (Crevecoeur and colleagues) found healed and unhealed injuries on 41 of them (about two-thirds), many caused by stone-tipped arrows or spears, and a best date estimate of roughly 13,400–13,700 years ago. It is often cited as the oldest known evidence of repeated, organised violence between groups.",
 [('One massacre','The original interpretation saw the cemetery as the result of a single battle or massacre.'),
  ('Recurrent raids','The 2021 study argues that the mix of healed and fresh wounds points instead to sporadic, repeated raids and ambushes over a person\'s lifetime.'),
  ('Climate stress','Since Hoffman (1993), researchers have linked the violence to competition for scarce resources as the climate and the Nile changed at the end of the last Ice Age; this remains a hypothesis rather than proven.'),
  ('How old is war?','The site is a key data point in the wider debate over whether organised warfare is ancient and widespread among hunter-gatherers or arose mainly with farming and states.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41598-021-89386-y','wiki:Jebel_Sahaba'],None,dateNote='Best estimate c. 13,400–13,700 years ago (older estimates up to c. 18,600)',
 changed='1964: cemetery found during the Nubian rescue campaign. 1960s–90s: read as a single battle. 2021: re-analysis argues for repeated violence and a younger date.'),
X('garamantes','The Garamantes: a Saharan kingdom that mined fossil water',H,'confirmed',2,-500,['archaeological','textual (classical)','remote sensing'],
 "The Garamantes lived in the Fezzan region of south-west Libya, in the middle of the Sahara, and are mentioned by Herodotus in the 5th century BCE as \"a very great nation\" who used four-horse chariots to hunt \"Troglodyte Ethiopians\". Their capital Garama (modern Germa) was excavated from the 1960s. They built an extensive network of underground channels (foggaras) to tap groundwater stored from wetter millennia, supporting farms, towns and trans-Saharan trade with the Roman world. Their state declined in late antiquity; most researchers connect the decline with falling groundwater levels among other factors.",
 [('Herodotus\'s wonders','Herodotus also tells of Garamantian oxen that grazed walking backwards because their horns curved forward; scholars debate how much of his account is reliable report and how much traveller\'s tale. Some read the chariot raids on \"Troglodytes\" as early slave raiding, others as a speculative tale.'),
  ('Why the decline?','Explanations include depletion of non-renewable fossil water, shifting trade routes, and political changes in late antiquity and after the Arab conquests of the 7th century; the weighting is debated.'),
  ('Lessons for today','The Garamantes are often cited in discussions of modern \"fossil water\" use, including Libya\'s Great Man-Made River project; this is an analogy, not a historical claim.')],
 ['wiki:Garamantes','wiki:Germa'],'Germa',end=700,dateNote='Kingdom c. 500 BCE – c. 700 CE (roots earlier)'),
X('carthage-tophet','The Carthage Tophet: child sacrifice, or a cemetery for infants?',H,'disputed',2,-750,['archaeological','skeletal analysis','textual (classical and biblical)','inscriptions'],
 "The Tophet of Salammbô at Carthage (in modern Tunisia) is a sacred precinct used from about the 8th century BCE until the Roman destruction of Carthage in 146 BCE. It contains thousands of urns holding the cremated remains of infants and young animals, many marked by stone stelae with dedications to the god Baal Hammon and the goddess Tanit recording a vow, often using the term mlk (molk). Greek and Roman writers such as Diodorus Siculus describe Carthaginians sacrificing children, especially in crises, and the Hebrew Bible condemns child sacrifice at a \"Topheth\" near Jerusalem.",
 [('Ritual sacrifice','Many archaeologists and historians argue that the inscriptions, the classical texts and the age profile of the remains together show that children were offered to the gods in fulfilment of vows (Smith and colleagues, Xella and others).'),
  ('Cemetery for the very young','An osteological study by Schwartz and colleagues (2010) argued that many of the infants died before or around birth, suggesting a special burial ground for children who died naturally, possibly with rituals attached.'),
  ('Enemy propaganda?','Because most written accounts come from Carthage\'s enemies (Greeks, Romans) or from biblical polemic, some scholars see them as exaggerated; others reply that the Punic inscriptions themselves describe offerings.')],
 ['doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0009177','doi:10.1017/s0003598x00047888','doi:10.15184/aqy.2016.270','wiki:Tophet'],None,end=-146,dateNote='c. 8th century BCE – 146 BCE'),
X('akhenaten','Akhenaten: the pharaoh who replaced the gods, and was erased',H,'confirmed',1,-1353,['archaeological','textual','genetic','art historical'],
 "Amenhotep IV came to the Egyptian throne around 1353 BCE, renamed himself Akhenaten (\"effective for the Aten\") and promoted the worship of the Aten, the sun disc, above or instead of Egypt's other gods. He founded a new capital, Akhetaten (modern Amarna), and art of his reign showed the royal family with elongated faces, skulls and bellies. After his death the court returned to the old gods; his son Tutankhaten became Tutankhamun, Amarna was abandoned, and later rulers erased Akhenaten's name, calling him \"the enemy\". Much of what is known was recovered only from the 19th century onwards, including the Amarna diplomatic letters found in the 1880s.",
 [('First monotheist?','Some scholars see Atenism as the first known monotheism; others call it henotheism or a royal cult centred on the king as the Aten\'s sole intermediary. Sigmund Freud (Moses and Monotheism, 1939) speculated that Moses was an Atenist priest; most historians reject this as unsupported.'),
  ('Art or illness?','His unusual portraits have been explained as religious artistic convention, or as signs of a medical condition such as Marfan syndrome; DNA and CT studies of the Tutankhamun family (2010) reported no evidence of Marfan syndrome in the mummy from tomb KV55, whose identification as Akhenaten is itself debated.'),
  ('Alien-hybrid claims (speculative)','Some popular writers have claimed the elongated depictions show a non-human or alien lineage; there is no evidence for this, and Egyptologists attribute the style to Amarna art conventions.')],
 ['doi:10.1001/jama.2010.121','wiki:Akhenaten','url:https://www.amarnaproject.com/|The Amarna Project (University of Cambridge-linked excavation)|project'],'Akhenaten',end=-1336,dateNote='Reigned c. 1353–1336 BCE'),
X('pharaohs-curse','The curse of Tutankhamun: a newspaper legend tested by statistics',H,'debunked',2,1923,['textual (press)','epidemiological study'],
 "Howard Carter's team opened Tutankhamun's tomb in the Valley of the Kings in November 1922. When the expedition's sponsor Lord Carnarvon died in Cairo in April 1923 – from blood poisoning after an infected mosquito bite was nicked while shaving – newspapers spread the idea of a pharaoh's curse. The novelist Marie Corelli had published a warning shortly before, and Arthur Conan Doyle suggested \"elementals\" guarding the tomb. Each later death linked to the excavation fed the story. No curse inscription was found in the tomb.",
 [('Curse','The legend holds that disturbing the tomb brought death on those involved; it became a fixture of films and fiction.'),
  ('Statistical test','A 2002 BMJ cohort study by Mark Nelson compared Westerners present at key openings with those who were not, and found no significant difference in survival; Carter himself lived until 1939.'),
  ('Natural hazards','Some have suggested moulds or bacteria in sealed tombs could harm visitors; there is no evidence that this caused Carnarvon\'s death.'),
  ('Media creation','Historians note that the curse story was amplified by rivalry between newspapers, after Carnarvon sold exclusive rights to The Times.')],
 ['doi:10.1136/bmj.325.7378.1482','wiki:Curse_of_the_pharaohs'],'Curse_of_the_pharaohs',dateNote='Tomb opened 1922; Carnarvon died April 1923'),
X('taung-child','The Taung Child: Africa\'s first ape-man, rejected because of a hoax',H,'confirmed',1,-2800000,['fossil','comparative anatomy'],
 "In 1924 quarry workers at the Buxton Limeworks at Taung, South Africa, found a small fossil skull that reached anatomist Raymond Dart in Johannesburg. In 1925 Dart described it in Nature as Australopithecus africanus, a small-brained, upright ancestor linking apes and humans, and argued that humanity originated in Africa. Leading British scientists, including Arthur Keith, rejected his claims, partly because they expected an early large brain like that of Piltdown Man – later exposed (1953) as a hoax. Further finds by Robert Broom and others from the 1930s–40s vindicated Dart. The child is now thought to be roughly 2.8 million years old.",
 [('Dart vindicated','Today Australopithecus is accepted as a hominin genus and Africa as the birthplace of humankind; the Taung case is a textbook example of how expectations shaped the reception of evidence.'),
  ('Killed by an eagle','In 2006 Lee Berger and Ron Clarke argued, from puncture marks in the eye sockets, that the child was killed by a large bird of prey; this is widely accepted though still discussed.'),
  ('Asia-first theories','Before Taung many researchers favoured an Asian origin for humans; that view persisted until more African fossils accumulated in the mid-20th century.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/115195a0','wiki:Taung_Child'],'Taung_Child',dateNote='c. 2.8 million years ago; found 1924, described 1925',
 changed='1925: Dart names Australopithecus and is widely rejected. 1940s: further finds turn opinion. 1953: Piltdown exposed. 2006: eagle-predation hypothesis.'),
X('san-rock-art','San rock art: trance, rain-animals and the spirit world of southern Africa',H,'confirmed',2,-3000,['rock art','ethnography','oral tradition','radiocarbon'],
 "Tens of thousands of rock paintings and engravings across southern Africa – in the Drakensberg, the Cederberg, Namibia, Botswana and Zimbabwe – were made by the ancestors of San hunter-gatherers over thousands of years, with some made into the 19th century. Common subjects include eland, human figures, dances and part-human, part-animal beings. In the 1870s Wilhelm Bleek and Lucy Lloyd recorded extensive testimony from /Xam San informants in Cape Town, which later scholars used to interpret the images, including accounts of rain-making in which trance specialists \"captured\" a rain-animal whose blood and milk became rain.",
 [('Trance and the spirit world','From the 1970s–80s David Lewis-Williams and others argued that much of the art depicts the experiences of healers in trance dances – sensations, transformations into animals and journeys to the spirit world – an interpretation now widely influential.'),
  ('Myth and narrative','Other scholars (for example Anne Solomon) argue the trance model is over-applied and that many images depict myths, the spirits of the dead or narrative scenes.'),
  ('Shared human patterns (debated)','The neuropsychological model has been extended to European Ice Age caves, suggesting common human trance imagery; critics question applying southern African ethnography to such distant times and places.')],
 ['wiki:San_rock_art','url:https://www.wits.ac.za/rockart/|Rock Art Research Institute, University of the Witwatersrand|institution'],'San_rock_art',end=1880,dateNote='Tradition spans thousands of years into the 19th century; start date indicative'),
X('dahomey-amazons','The Agojie: the women soldiers of Dahomey',H,'confirmed',2,1700,['textual (European and oral accounts)','photographs','oral tradition'],
 "The Kingdom of Dahomey (in present-day Benin) maintained an all-female military corps, called Agojie or Mino (\"our mothers\") in Fon, from at least the 18th century until the kingdom was conquered by France in 1894. Traditions trace the corps to King Houegbadja or to a corps of female elephant hunters (gbeto) and palace guards. At its height it may have numbered several thousand; the women were formally married to the king and lived in the palace. They fought neighbouring states, including the Egba at Abeokuta, and the French in 1890–92. A woman named Nawi, interviewed in 1978 and thought to be the last veteran, died in 1979.",
 [('Origins debated','Explanations include a shortage of men after wars and slave raids, the king\'s need for loyal guards inside the palace, and older traditions of women hunters.'),
  ('Amazons named by outsiders','European visitors called them \"Amazons\" after Greek myth, shaping how they were described; Fon terms and oral histories give a different picture.'),
  ('Role in the slave trade','Dahomey was deeply involved in the Atlantic slave trade, and the Agojie took part in raids that captured people for sale; this was debated publicly around the 2022 film The Woman King.')],
 ['wiki:Dahomey_Amazons','url:https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/the-real-warrior-women-behind-the-woman-king-180980750/|Smithsonian Magazine: The Real History Behind The Woman King|magazine'],'Dahomey_Amazons',end=1894,dateNote='At least 18th century – 1894 (traditions trace them to the 17th)'),
X('nzinga','Queen Nzinga of Ndongo and Matamba: diplomat, warrior and legend',H,'confirmed',2,1622,['textual (Portuguese, missionary)','oral tradition'],
 "Nzinga Mbande (c. 1583–1663) ruled the Mbundu kingdoms of Ndongo (from 1624) and Matamba (from 1631) in present-day Angola. As envoy to the Portuguese governor in Luanda in 1622 she negotiated a peace treaty; a famous story says that, offered only a mat while the Portuguese sat on chairs, she had an attendant kneel to form a seat. She later allied with the Dutch West India Company, fought the Portuguese for decades, and made peace in 1656, dying in 1663. She is a national heroine in Angola.",
 [('Diplomat and strategist','Historians emphasise her use of baptism, alliances (including with Imbangala war bands) and negotiation to keep her kingdoms independent.'),
  ('Hostile sources','Much of her life is known from Capuchin missionaries such as Antonio Cavazzi, whose accounts include lurid stories of cruelty and of a male harem dressed as women; scholars treat these with caution as possibly shaped by European prejudice.'),
  ('Symbol','After independence (1975) she became a symbol of resistance to colonial rule; some critics note that, like many rulers of the time, she also took part in the slave trade.')],
 ['wiki:Nzinga_of_Ndongo_and_Matamba'],'Nzinga_of_Ndongo_and_Matamba',end=1663,dateNote='Embassy 1622; reigned 1624–1663'),
X('nongqawuse','Nongqawuse and the Xhosa cattle-killing: a prophecy that the dead would rise',H,'confirmed',1,1856,['textual (colonial records, depositions)','oral tradition'],
 "In April 1856 Nongqawuse, a Xhosa girl of about 15 living near the mouth of the Gxarha River (in the present Eastern Cape, South Africa), said she had met the spirits of ancestors. Her message, relayed by her uncle Mhlakaza and accepted by the paramount chief Sarili, was that if the people killed their cattle and destroyed their crops the dead would rise, healthy new cattle would appear and the colonists would be swept into the sea. The Xhosa were already suffering land loss after frontier wars and an epidemic of bovine lungsickness. Over 1856–57 some 300,000–400,000 cattle were killed; when the prophesied day passed, famine killed about 40,000 people, and the colonial governor Sir George Grey used the crisis to seize land and recruit labour.",
 [('Millenarian movement','Historian Jeff Peires (The Dead Will Arise, 1989) interprets the movement as a rational response within Xhosa belief to disease and colonial pressure, comparable to other movements expecting the return of the dead.'),
  ('Conspiracy explanations','A belief that Grey or missionaries engineered the prophecy to destroy the Xhosa has circulated; historians generally find no evidence of a plot, though Grey clearly exploited the aftermath.'),
  ('Believers and unbelievers','Communities split between believers and \"amagogotya\" (unbelievers) who refused; the failure was blamed on them, a pattern seen in other prophecy movements.')],
 ['wiki:Nongqawuse'],'Nongqawuse',end=1858),
# --- part 2: Mesoamerica and Native North America
X('cholula','The Great Pyramid of Cholula: the world\'s largest pyramid, hidden under a hill and a church',H,'confirmed',1,-300,['archaeological','textual (colonial)','mythological'],
 "The Great Pyramid of Cholula, or Tlachihualtepetl (\"made mountain\" in Nahuatl), in Puebla, Mexico, is the largest pyramid known by volume. It was built in successive stages from about the 3rd century BCE to the 9th century CE and was later dedicated to Quetzalcoatl. By the Spanish conquest it was overgrown and looked like a natural hill; in 1594 the Spanish built the church of Nuestra Señora de los Remedios on top. Tunnels dug by archaeologists from the 1930s revealed the inner structures.",
 [('A giant\'s tower to the sky','A tradition recorded in the 1560s by the Dominican friar Pedro de los Ríos (Codex Vaticanus A) and popularised by Alexander von Humboldt says that after a great flood one of seven surviving giants, Xelhua, built the pyramid in memory of the mountain that saved them; the gods, angered that it would reach the clouds, hurled fire and halted the work.'),
  ('Babel echo?','Humboldt and later writers noted the parallel with the Tower of Babel. Many historians suspect the colonial versions mixed native tradition with biblical stories taught by missionaries; others hold that a pre-conquest core existed.'),
  ('Hidden in plain sight','The pyramid\'s burial under earth and vegetation, and the church on its summit, are interpreted variously as natural overgrowth, deliberate concealment, or Christian appropriation of a sacred place.')],
 ['wiki:Great_Pyramid_of_Cholula','url:https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Vues_des_Cordill%C3%A8res/T1/8|Humboldt, Vues des Cordillères (1810), chapter on the pyramid of Cholula (Wikisource, French)|primary text','url:https://elementos.buap.mx/post.php?id=479|Elementos (BUAP): Cholula, gigantes y Xelhua – exégesis de una placa conmemorativa|article'],'Great_Pyramid_of_Cholula',end=900,dateNote='Built c. 3rd century BCE – 9th century CE'),
X('tenochtitlan-founding','Eagle on a cactus: the founding of Tenochtitlan',M,'myth-legend',1,1325,['textual (codices)','archaeological','mythological'],
 "According to Mexica tradition, the people migrating from Aztlan were told by their god Huitzilopochtli to settle where they saw an eagle perched on a prickly-pear cactus (nopal) growing from a rock. They found the sign on an island in Lake Texcoco and founded Tenochtitlan there, traditionally in 1325 (the date 13 March 1325 was fixed in 1925 for a 600th-anniversary celebration). Tenochtitlan grew into a city of perhaps 200,000 people before the Spanish conquest of 1521; Mexico City was built over it and the lake was largely drained. The eagle and cactus are on Mexico's flag.",
 [('Founding myth','The story follows a widespread pattern of a divinely guided migration ending at a promised sign.'),
  ('The serpent question','In the best-known modern image the eagle devours a serpent. Some early depictions, such as the Codex Mendoza, show the eagle on the cactus without a snake; scholars suggest the serpent may reflect a later reading of the Aztec war glyph atl-tlachinolli (water and fire), or a blending with European symbolism.'),
  ('History behind the myth','Archaeologists and historians debate the real founding date and whether the island was already settled; the official story was also shaped by later Mexica rulers who reportedly rewrote their history.')],
 ['wiki:Tenochtitlan'],'Tenochtitlan',dateNote='Traditional date 1325'),
X('la-llorona','La Llorona: the weeping woman of the waters',M,'myth-legend',2,1550,['oral tradition','textual (colonial chronicles)','mythological'],
 "La Llorona (\"the weeping woman\") is a ghost of Mexican and wider Latin American folklore: a woman who drowned her children and now wanders by rivers and lakes at night crying for them, and who may seize anyone who hears her. Versions are found from the American Southwest to South America and are often used as warnings to children. Earlier roots are often traced to the Florentine Codex (compiled by Bernardino de Sahagún in the 16th century), which lists among the omens before the Spanish conquest a woman heard weeping at night for her children.",
 [('Aztec roots','Many folklorists connect her with the goddess Cihuacoatl and with Mexica beliefs about women who died in childbirth, whose spirits wandered at crossroads.'),
  ('La Malinche','She is sometimes conflated with Malintzin (La Malinche), the Nahua interpreter and mother of a son of Hernán Cortés, making the legend an allegory of conquest and betrayal.'),
  ('European parallels','Others note similarities with European tales of white ladies and water spirits, and see the modern legend as a colonial blend.')],
 ['wiki:La_Llorona'],'La_Llorona'),
X('spiro-mounds','Spiro Mounds: the looting of "America\'s King Tut\'s tomb"',H,'confirmed',2,1936,['archaeological','museum collections','textual'],
 "Spiro, in eastern Oklahoma, was a major centre of the Mississippian world from about 800 to 1450 CE, with a ceremonial complex of earthen mounds. In 1936 a group of men leased the site and formed the Pocola Mining Company, tunnelling into Craig Mound, where they found an unusual hollow chamber with extraordinarily preserved objects – engraved shells, copper plates, textiles, pearls, effigy pipes and ceremonial weapons. They sold the finds to collectors around the world, and the looting prompted Oklahoma to pass an antiquities law; professional excavations followed. Craig Mound has been called \"an American King Tut's tomb\".",
 [('Lost knowledge','Archaeologists consider the looting one of the worst in North American archaeology, because the context that would explain the objects was destroyed; objects are scattered across many museums.'),
  ('Descendant communities','The Caddo and Wichita nations are regarded as descendants of Spiro\'s people; their perspectives on the site and its objects are part of modern interpretation.'),
  ('Myths in shell','The engraved shell art, with winged beings, horned serpents and birdmen, is studied as a record of Mississippian cosmology; readings of specific figures differ.')],
 ['wiki:Spiro_Mounds','url:https://www.okhistory.org/sites/spiromounds|Oklahoma Historical Society: Spiro Mounds Archaeological Center|institution'],'Spiro_Mounds',dateNote='Site c. 800–1450 CE; looted 1936'),
X('skinwalker','Skin-walkers: a Navajo belief and a ranch of strange claims',M,'myth-legend',2,1996,['oral tradition','eyewitness testimony','speculative'],
 "In Navajo (Diné) tradition a yee naaldlooshii (\"by means of it, it goes on all fours\") is one kind of malevolent witch, believed able to transform into or disguise themselves as animals; the term is never used for healers. Traditional Navajo people are reluctant to discuss this lore with outsiders. In 1996 claims about strange lights, cattle mutilations and creatures on the Sherman Ranch in Utah's Uintah Basin – later called Skinwalker Ranch – were published in the Deseret News; Robert Bigelow bought the ranch that year to study them, and it has since been the subject of books, US government-funded research contracts and television series.",
 [('Cultural belief','For many Navajo, skin-walkers are part of a living religious and moral tradition; some, including Native writers, object to outsiders\' commercial and fictional use of it.'),
  ('Paranormal hot spot','Researchers associated with the ranch report unexplained lights, animal deaths and other phenomena, and describe it as a site of interest for UFO and paranormal study.'),
  ('Sceptical view','Critics note the lack of verifiable evidence and suggest misperception, predators, folklore and the incentives of publicity and television; no claim has been independently confirmed.')],
 ['wiki:Skin-walker','wiki:Skinwalker_Ranch'],'Skinwalker_Ranch',dateNote='Belief traditional; ranch claims from 1996'),
X('ghost-dance','The Ghost Dance: a vision during an eclipse, and Wounded Knee',H,'confirmed',1,1889,['textual','ethnography','oral tradition'],
 "On 1 January 1889, during a solar eclipse, the Northern Paiute spiritual leader Wovoka (Jack Wilson) in Nevada had a vision. He taught that performing a circle dance and living peacefully and honestly would reunite the living with the spirits of the dead, restore the land and end white expansion. The Ghost Dance spread rapidly among Plains peoples. Among the Lakota, the movement – with \"ghost shirts\" rumoured to stop bullets – alarmed US authorities; the attempt to suppress it led to the killing of Sitting Bull and, on 29 December 1890, the Wounded Knee massacre, in which US soldiers killed some 250–300 Lakota, many of them women and children. An earlier Ghost Dance had arisen among the Paiute around 1870.",
 [('Religious revival','Anthropologist James Mooney (1896) and later scholars describe it as a revitalisation movement responding to loss of land, bison and population.'),
  ('A threat?','Officials at the time portrayed it as a war movement; historians mostly see the panic as a misreading of a largely peaceful religion, with the ghost-shirt belief a Lakota development.'),
  ('A global pattern','Scholars compare it with other movements that promised the return of the dead or of ancestors, such as the Xhosa cattle-killing and Melanesian cargo cults.')],
 ['wiki:Ghost_Dance','wiki:Wovoka'],'Ghost_Dance',end=1890),
X('white-sands-footprints','White Sands footprints: people in North America 21,000 years ago?',H,'disputed',1,-21000,['fossil trackways','radiocarbon','luminescence dating'],
 "Human footprints preserved in former lake-shore sediments of Lake Otero in White Sands National Park, New Mexico, were identified from 2009 onwards. In 2021 Matthew Bennett and colleagues reported in Science that seeds of the aquatic plant Ruppia in layers above and below the prints dated them to about 21,000–23,000 years ago, during the Last Glacial Maximum – thousands of years before the long-favoured Clovis-first date of about 13,000 years. The tracks, mostly of teenagers and children, appear alongside those of mammoths and giant ground sloths; one set shows a person carrying a small child. A 2023 study using conifer pollen and quartz luminescence dates supported the older ages.",
 [('An early arrival','If correct, people were south of the ice sheets during the height of the Ice Age, implying an even earlier entry into the Americas.'),
  ('Dating sceptics','Critics argued that the aquatic seeds may have absorbed old carbon from groundwater, making them seem too old, and questioned the stratigraphy; debate continues on some of these points.'),
  ('Wider debate','White Sands joins other contested early sites (such as Monte Verde and Chiquihuite Cave) in debates over when and how the Americas were first peopled.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.abg7586','doi:10.1126/science.adh5007','wiki:White_Sands_footprints'],None,dateNote='c. 23,000–21,000 years ago (contested)',
 changed='2009: tracks recognised. 2021: dated to 21,000–23,000 years ago. 2022: reservoir-effect critiques. 2023: independent dates support the older age.'),
X('piasa','The Piasa: a painted monster on the Mississippi bluffs',M,'myth-legend',2,1673,['textual (explorer accounts)','rock art (lost)','mythological'],
 "In 1673 the French explorers Jacques Marquette and Louis Jolliet, travelling down the Mississippi near present-day Alton, Illinois, described two painted monsters on the river bluffs, with horns like a deer, red eyes, a human-like face and beard, a scaly body and a long tail ending in a fish's fin. Sketches from 1678 and 1682 survive. The bluff face carrying the painting was quarried away in the 19th century (around 1846–47 according to one account); a modern reconstruction now stands nearby.",
 [('Underwater panther','Many scholars relate the image to the underwater panther or horned serpent of regional Native traditions and to Mississippian iconography from nearby Cahokia.'),
  ('A 19th-century legend','The popular story of the \"Piasa bird\" that devoured people until the chief Ouatoga offered himself as bait was published by John Russell in 1836; later researchers concluded it was largely Russell\'s literary invention rather than recorded folklore.'),
  ('Dragon or bird?','The creature has been redrawn over time as a winged dragon-like bird, illustrating how a lost image can change as it is copied.')],
 ['wiki:Piasa'],'Piasa',dateNote='Described 1673; original destroyed in the 19th century'),
# --- part 3: Eastern Europe, the Slavic world and the medieval Islamic world
X('tartaria-tablets','The Tărtăria tablets: the world\'s oldest writing, or not writing at all?',H,'disputed',2,-5300,['archaeological','epigraphic','contested stratigraphy'],
 "In 1961 the archaeologist Nicolae Vlassa found three small clay tablets incised with symbols at Tărtăria in Transylvania, Romania, in a pit with burnt human bones and figurines. They belong to the world of the Vinča culture of south-eastern Europe, whose pottery and figurines carry a repertoire of signs (the Vinča symbols). If the tablets date to around 5300 BCE, as indirect dating of the associated layers suggests, they are older than the earliest Mesopotamian proto-writing.",
 [('Not writing','Most linguists and archaeologists regard the Vinča signs as symbols, marks of ownership or religious notation rather than a system that recorded language.'),
  ('Proto-writing (\"Danube script\")','Researchers such as Harald Haarmann and Marco Merlini argue the signs form an early writing or proto-writing system of \"Old Europe\".'),
  ('Dating dispute','Because the tablets were baked to conserve them, they cannot be dated directly, and the excavation\'s stratigraphy has been questioned; early on, some scholars instead compared them with Mesopotamian tablets of around 3000 BCE.')],
 ['wiki:Tărtăria_tablets','wiki:Vinča_symbols'],'Tărtăria_tablets',dateNote='c. 5300 BCE by indirect dating (disputed)'),
X('cucuteni-trypillia','Cucuteni–Trypillia: Europe\'s first giant settlements, burned and rebuilt',H,'confirmed',2,-5050,['archaeological','geophysical survey','radiocarbon'],
 "The Cucuteni–Trypillia culture flourished from roughly 5000 to 3000 BCE (estimates vary; recent ones give c. 5050–2950 BCE) in what are now Romania, Moldova and Ukraine. Around 4100–3600 BCE some of its settlements, such as Talianki and Maidanetske in Ukraine, grew into \"mega-sites\" covering more than 100 hectares – Talianki around 335 hectares – with up to a few thousand houses arranged in concentric ovals, among the largest settlements in the world at the time and earlier than the first Mesopotamian cities. A striking feature is that many houses were burned, apparently in a repeated cycle, and settlements were then rebuilt.",
 [('Deliberate burning','Many archaeologists think houses were burned on purpose, perhaps as a ritual at the end of a house\'s life or a settlement cycle.'),
  ('Accident or war','Others suggest accidental fires or attacks; experiments show burning a wattle-and-daub house so thoroughly requires extra fuel, which supports the deliberate view but does not settle the reason.'),
  ('Cities without kings?','The mega-sites show little sign of palaces, temples or a ruling elite, and are debated as possible early \"egalitarian\" or low-hierarchy cities, or as seasonal gathering places rather than permanent towns.'),
  ('Old Europe','Marija Gimbutas saw the culture as part of a peaceful, goddess-centred \"Old Europe\" overrun by steppe peoples; this view is influential but widely criticised as too idealised.')],
 ['doi:10.1007/s10963-017-9105-8','wiki:Cucuteni–Trypillia_culture'],'Cucuteni–Trypillia_culture',end=-2950),
X('kitezh','Kitezh: the Russian city that sank to escape the Mongols',M,'myth-legend',2,1238,['textual (legend chronicle)','oral tradition','mythological'],
 "The legend of Kitezh tells of a city founded by Grand Prince Georgy (Yuri) II of Vladimir that, when the army of Batu Khan attacked during the Mongol invasion of the 13th century, was saved by being submerged in, or made invisible beneath, Lake Svetloyar near Nizhny Novgorod. Only the righteous can see it; pilgrims report hearing its church bells and singing from under the water. The legend survives mainly in the \"Kitezh Chronicle\", copied by Old Believers in the late 18th century, and inspired Rimsky-Korsakov's opera The Legend of the Invisible City of Kitezh (1907).",
 [('Sacred lake','Folklorists see the story as part of Russian Orthodox and Old Believer tradition of a hidden holy city, and pilgrims still walk around the lake on its shores at night.'),
  ('Real ruins?','Expeditions have searched Svetloyar; geological studies suggest the lake formed long before, and no submerged city has been found, though some local traditions point to older settlement remains in the area.'),
  ('Sunken-city pattern','Kitezh is often called the \"Russian Atlantis\" and is compared with Ys in Brittany and other drowned cities whose bells are heard under the water.')],
 ['wiki:Kitezh'],None,dateNote='Legend set in 1238 (Mongol invasion); text late 18th century'),
X('baba-yaga','Baba Yaga: the witch in the hut on chicken legs',M,'myth-legend',2,1755,['oral tradition','textual (folklore collections)','mythological'],
 "Baba Yaga is one of the best-known figures of Slavic folklore: an old woman who lives deep in the forest in a hut standing on chicken legs, flies in a mortar steered with a pestle and sweeps away her tracks with a broom. In folk tales she may eat children, or test and help heroes and heroines who come to her. The first clear written mention is in Mikhail Lomonosov's Russian Grammar of 1755; most tales were collected in the 19th century, notably by Alexander Afanasyev.",
 [('Initiation figure','The folklorist Vladimir Propp interpreted Baba Yaga and her hut as memories of initiation rites and of the boundary between the living and the dead.'),
  ('Goddess remnant?','Some scholars see her as a survival of a pre-Christian goddess of death, birth or nature; others caution that there is no early evidence for such a cult.'),
  ('Ambiguity','Her double nature as dangerous ogress and wise helper is widely discussed and has made her a popular figure in modern fiction.')],
 ['wiki:Baba_Yaga'],'Baba_Yaga',dateNote='First clear mention 1755; folk roots older'),
X('arnold-paole','Arnold Paole: the Serbian vampire case that alarmed Europe',H,'confirmed',2,1726,['textual (official military-medical report)','folk belief'],
 "Arnold Paole was a Serbian hajduk (soldier) in the village of Medveđa, then under Habsburg rule, who said he had once been troubled by a vampire and had protected himself by eating earth from its grave. After he died from a fall from a hay wagon around 1726, villagers blamed him for several deaths; his body was exhumed, said to look undecayed with fresh blood, and staked. In 1731–32, after further deaths, Austrian military doctors including Johann Flückinger investigated and their report, Visum et Repertum, dated 26 January 1732, described exhumed bodies that seemed uncorrupted. It was widely published and helped make the word \"vampire\" known across Western Europe.",
 [('Folk belief','Villagers interpreted the deaths through Balkan beliefs in the restless dead who return to drain the living.'),
  ('Decomposition explained','Researchers such as Paul Barber (Vampires, Burial and Death, 1988) argue that the signs described – bloated bodies, blood at the mouth, apparent growth of nails, sounds when staked – match normal processes of decomposition.'),
  ('Epidemic','The cluster of deaths has been attributed to an infectious disease; which one is unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Arnold_Paole'],None,dateNote='Died c. 1726; report 1732'),
X('anna-anderson','Anna Anderson: the woman who claimed to be Grand Duchess Anastasia',H,'debunked',2,1920,['textual','court records','DNA'],
 "In 1920 a young woman was pulled from a canal in Berlin after a suicide attempt and later claimed to be Grand Duchess Anastasia, youngest daughter of Tsar Nicholas II, who was said to have survived the 1918 killing of the Romanov family. As Anna Anderson she drew supporters and opponents among Russian émigrés and royalty; a 1927 investigation identified her as Franziska Schanzkowska, a Polish factory worker who had disappeared in Berlin. A German court case lasting decades ended without a ruling that she was Anastasia. She later lived in Charlottesville, Virginia, and died in 1984. After the Romanov remains were found, DNA tests in 1994 on preserved tissue showed she was not a Romanov and matched a relative of Schanzkowska; the remains of the last two missing children were identified in 2008–09.",
 [('Survival legend','Rumours that one or more of the tsar\'s children escaped circulated for decades, encouraged by the secrecy over the burial site and by many claimants.'),
  ('DNA verdict','Scientists regard the DNA evidence as conclusive that Anderson was not Anastasia and that all of the family died in 1918.'),
  ('Lingering doubts','A few supporters questioned the chain of custody of the tested samples; tests on several independent samples gave the same result.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/ng0294-130','doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0004838','wiki:Anna_Anderson'],'Anna_Anderson',end=1984),
X('house-of-wisdom','The House of Wisdom: Baghdad\'s translation movement',H,'confirmed',1,800,['textual'],
 "The Bayt al-Hikma, or House of Wisdom, was a library and centre of learning in Abbasid Baghdad, associated with the caliphs Harun al-Rashid and especially al-Ma'mun (reigned 813–833). It was linked to the Graeco-Arabic translation movement, in which works of Greek philosophy, medicine, mathematics and astronomy, along with Persian, Indian (Sanskrit) and Syriac texts, were translated into Arabic. Scholars connected to the movement include al-Khwarizmi, whose name gave us \"algorithm\", and the translator Hunayn ibn Ishaq. Baghdad was sacked by the Mongols in 1258, and later accounts say the Tigris ran black with the ink of destroyed books.",
 [('Golden-age academy','A popular modern image presents the House of Wisdom as a great university or academy that preserved ancient learning and launched an Islamic scientific golden age.'),
  ('A palace library?','Historians such as Dimitri Gutas argue that the translation movement was driven by many patrons across Baghdad\'s elite, and that the Bayt al-Hikma itself was probably a palace library on a Sasanian model rather than a research institution; its role has been mythologised.'),
  ('Lost libraries','The story of the 1258 destruction is often paired with that of the Library of Alexandria as a symbol of knowledge lost to war; scholars note that both stories have been dramatised.')],
 ['wiki:House_of_Wisdom'],'House_of_Wisdom',end=1258,dateNote='c. 800s; Baghdad sacked 1258'),
X('ibn-fadlan','Ibn Fadlan meets the Rus: a ship burning on the Volga',H,'confirmed',2,922,['textual (eyewitness account)'],
 "Ahmad ibn Fadlan was a member of an embassy sent in 921 by the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadir from Baghdad to the king of the Volga Bulgars, arriving in May 922. His account (the Risala) describes the peoples he met, including traders he called the Rus, generally identified as Scandinavians (Vikings). It contains a vivid description of the funeral of a Rus chieftain, in which an enslaved girl is killed and burned with him in his ship. A fuller manuscript was found in Mashhad, Iran, in 1923. Michael Crichton drew on it for his novel Eaters of the Dead (1976), filmed as The 13th Warrior (1999).",
 [('Rare eyewitness','Historians value the Risala as one of very few outsider accounts of Viking-age Scandinavians abroad, matching archaeological finds of ship burials.'),
  ('Outsider bias','Others caution that Ibn Fadlan observed through the lens of his own culture and may have misunderstood rituals; whether the Rus he met were Scandinavian, Slavic or mixed is debated.'),
  ('Fiction blending','Crichton\'s novel mixed the real account with the Beowulf story, and the film version has sometimes been taken as history by audiences.')],
 ['wiki:Ahmad_ibn_Fadlan'],None,dateNote='Embassy 921–922'),
X('seven-sleepers','The Seven Sleepers of Ephesus: a cave sleep of centuries',M,'myth-legend',2,250,['textual (Christian and Islamic scripture)','mythological','pilgrimage sites'],
 "A legend told by Christians and Muslims says that during the persecution of the Roman emperor Decius (around 250 CE) a group of young Christian men hid in a cave near Ephesus and fell into a miraculous sleep, waking centuries later – Christian versions place it under the Christian emperor Theodosius II – to find their faith triumphant. Early versions in Syriac appear in the 5th–6th centuries, and the story is told in the Qur'an (Surah 18, al-Kahf, \"The Cave\"), which includes their dog and says they stayed \"three hundred years, and add nine\".",
 [('Faith and resurrection','In Christian tradition the story was read as proof of bodily resurrection; in Islam, as a sign of God\'s power and of protection for believers.'),
  ('Many caves','Several places claim the cave, including Ephesus in Turkey, a site near Amman in Jordan and Tarsus; each has become a place of pilgrimage.'),
  ('Time-slip pattern','The story belongs to a widespread family of tales in which a person sleeps or visits another world and returns after long years, such as Japan\'s Urashima Tarō, Ireland\'s Oisín and Washington Irving\'s Rip Van Winkle.')],
 ['wiki:Seven_Sleepers'],'Seven_Sleepers',dateNote='Set c. 250 CE; texts from 5th–7th centuries'),
X('roc-elephant-bird','The roc and the elephant bird: a giant bird of legend and a real one',M,'myth-legend',2,1298,['textual (travel accounts)','fossil','mythological'],
 "The roc is a gigantic bird of Middle Eastern legend, said to carry off elephants; it appears in the voyages of Sinbad in the One Thousand and One Nights. Marco Polo, around 1298, reported that the bird lived on islands south of Madagascar and that Kublai Khan had been shown a giant roc feather. Madagascar was home to the real elephant birds (Aepyornithidae), flightless birds up to about 3 metres tall that laid the largest eggs known; they died out within roughly the last thousand years, after people arrived. Huge eggs from Madagascar reached the French Academy of Sciences in 1851.",
 [('Memory of real birds','Some writers think the roc legend grew from sailors\' reports of elephant-bird eggs and bones, or of Madagascar\'s now-extinct giant eagles.'),
  ('Older myth','Others trace the roc to older traditions such as the Indian Garuda, the Persian Simurgh and the Arabian anqa, and note that a giant flightless bird cannot carry elephants.'),
  ('A real feather?','The \"roc feathers\" shown in China are often thought to have been fronds of the raffia palm; this is a plausible guess rather than documented.')],
 ['doi:10.1098/rsos.181295','wiki:Roc_(mythology)','wiki:Elephant_bird'],'Roc_(mythology)',dateNote='Marco Polo c. 1298; legend older'),
# --- part 4: Australia, 19th-century anomalies and the history of science
X('wolfe-creek','Wolfe Creek crater (Kandimalal): where the evening star fell',H,'confirmed',2,-120000,['geological','oral tradition','mythological'],
 "Wolfe Creek Crater in the Kimberley region of Western Australia is a well-preserved meteorite impact crater about 875 metres across. It was brought to scientists' attention after being spotted during an aerial survey in 1947 and was described in 1949. It was long thought to be about 300,000 years old, but a 2019 study estimated it to be less than 120,000 years old. Small iron meteorites and iron-rich \"shale balls\" have been found around it. The local Djaru (Jaru) people call it Kandimalal and have several Dreaming stories about its origin.",
 [('Dreaming stories','One story says the round crater was made by a rainbow snake emerging from the earth; another, told by an Elder, says that the evening star passed close to the crescent moon, became so hot that it fell to the ground with a great explosion, flash and dust cloud, and that people long feared to go near the place.'),
  ('Memory of an impact?','Some writers suggest the falling-star story may preserve a memory of the impact, since people have lived in Australia for perhaps 50,000–65,000 years; others note the story could have arisen after people saw the crater and that the impact\'s precise age is uncertain.'),
  ('Science and culture','The crater is a site of interest both for impact science and for discussions of how Aboriginal knowledge records landscape features.')],
 ['wiki:Wolfe_Creek_Crater'],'Wolfe_Creek_Crater',dateNote='Impact less than c. 120,000 years ago (2019 estimate)'),
X('mahogany-ship','The Mahogany Ship: a lost wreck in the dunes of Victoria',H,'disputed',3,1836,['textual (19th-century recollections)','folklore','speculative'],
 "The Mahogany Ship is a supposed old shipwreck said to lie buried in sand dunes in the Armstrong Bay area west of Warrnambool, Victoria, Australia. 19th-century settlers and whalers told of a wreck of dark timber; the \"1836 Hopkins River incident\", in which shipwrecked sealers reportedly saw an old wreck, became the story's foundation and was popularised by George Dunderdale's The Book of the Bush (1898). A Portland newspaper of 1847 described a wreck in the hummocks near Warrnambool. Searches since the 1980s, and symposia in 1981, 1987 and 2005, have found no conclusive evidence that such a wreck exists.",
 [('Portuguese caravel','In 1977 Kenneth McIntyre argued that it was a caravel from a secret Portuguese voyage under Cristóvão de Mendonça around 1522, part of his theory that the Portuguese reached Australia before the Dutch.'),
  ('Folklore','Researcher Jenny Fawcett and others argue that the accounts are inconsistent second-hand recollections and that the legend grew in retelling; some suggest a later wreck was misidentified.'),
  ('Other origins','Spanish, Dutch and Chinese origins have also been proposed; none has been confirmed.')],
 ['wiki:Mahogany_Ship'],None,dateNote='Earliest claimed sighting 1836'),
X('great-moon-hoax','The Great Moon Hoax: bat-people seen through a telescope',H,'debunked',2,1835,['textual (newspaper)'],
 "Beginning on 25 August 1835, the New York newspaper The Sun published a series of six articles claiming that the astronomer Sir John Herschel, working at the Cape of Good Hope, had used a powerful new telescope to discover life on the Moon, including bison, unicorn-like goats, beavers walking upright and winged humanoids (\"Vespertilio-homo\", bat-men) living beside temples. The supposed author, Dr Andrew Grant, was fictitious. The series was widely believed and reprinted, and boosted The Sun's circulation. On 16 September 1835 the paper admitted the articles were a hoax, and the reporter Richard Adams Locke publicly admitted authorship in 1840.",
 [('Satire and its times','Historians link the series to popular speculative science of the day, such as the Reverend Thomas Dick\'s claims that the Moon was inhabited by billions of beings, and some read it partly as satire; many readers took it literally.'),
  ('Media lesson','It is often cited as an early example of mass-media hoaxing and of how scientific authority (here, Herschel\'s name) can lend credibility to invented claims.'),
  ('Poe\'s claim','Edgar Allan Poe claimed the story plagiarised his own moon-voyage tale, \"Hans Pfaall\", published shortly before.')],
 ['wiki:Great_Moon_Hoax'],'Great_Moon_Hoax',dateNote='25 August – September 1835'),
X('mystery-airships','The phantom airships of 1896–97: UFOs before aircraft',H,'disputed',2,1896,['textual (newspaper reports)','eyewitness testimony'],
 "From 17 November 1896, starting in Sacramento, California, and continuing through mid-1897 across the Midwest and Texas, thousands of people in the United States reported seeing mysterious lighted airships in the night sky, years before the first successful powered aeroplane flights. Some newspaper reports described cigar-shaped craft with wings, propellers and searchlights, and encounters with pilots who claimed to be inventors. One of the most famous stories, published in April 1897, said an airship had crashed into a windmill at Aurora, Texas, and that its pilot, \"not an inhabitant of this world\", was buried in the town cemetery.",
 [('Hoaxes and misperception','Historians and sceptics attribute most reports to newspaper hoaxes, tall tales, misidentified stars and planets (such as Venus), balloons and the excitement of a period anticipating flight; the Aurora story is widely considered a hoax.'),
  ('Secret inventors','Some at the time believed a secret inventor had built a working airship; no evidence of such a craft has been found.'),
  ('Early UFO wave','UFO researchers regard the wave as an early example of unexplained aerial sightings and note how the objects matched the technology people then expected – airships – just as later sightings resembled rockets and saucers.')],
 ['wiki:Mystery_airship'],'Mystery_airship',end=1897),
X('fox-sisters','The Fox sisters: knocking spirits and the birth of Spiritualism',H,'debunked',2,1848,['textual','confessions','eyewitness testimony'],
 "In March 1848 in Hydesville, New York, the young sisters Kate and Margaretta (Maggie) Fox reported rapping sounds in their home, which they said were messages from a spirit, later identified as a murdered peddler. Managed by their elder sister Leah, they became famous mediums, and their séances helped launch the Spiritualist movement, which spread across the US and Europe. In 1851 doctors from Buffalo concluded the raps were made by cracking joints. In October 1888 Maggie publicly confessed at the New York Academy of Music, demonstrating how she made the sounds with her toe joints, and the confession was published in the New York World; in 1889 she retracted it.",
 [('Fraud','Most historians accept the 1888 confession and the earlier investigations as showing that the raps were produced by the sisters.'),
  ('Spiritualist view','Spiritualists argued that the confession was given for money (a reporter had offered a fee) and pointed to Maggie\'s retraction.'),
  ('The bones in the cellar','In 1904 human remains were reportedly found behind a fallen cellar wall of the house; supporters saw it as proof of the murdered peddler, while sceptics note that no investigation confirmed whose bones they were or how they got there.')],
 ['wiki:Fox_sisters'],'Fox_sisters',end=1888,dateNote='Rappings began March 1848; confession 1888'),
X('semmelweis','Ignaz Semmelweis: the doctor who said wash your hands, and was rejected',H,'confirmed',1,1847,['statistical records','textual'],
 "In 1847, at the Vienna General Hospital, the Hungarian doctor Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that women giving birth in the clinic staffed by doctors and medical students died of childbed (puerperal) fever far more often than those in the clinic staffed by midwives. After his colleague Jakob Kolletschka died of a similar illness following a scalpel cut during an autopsy, Semmelweis concluded that \"cadaverous particles\" on doctors' hands were transmitting the disease, and introduced handwashing in a chlorinated lime solution, which sharply reduced deaths. His ideas were widely rejected; he became increasingly angry, was committed to an asylum in 1865 and died there of an infected wound about two weeks later. The work of Louis Pasteur and Joseph Lister on germs later vindicated him.",
 [('Rejected pioneer','He is often presented as a martyr of science whose evidence was ignored because it contradicted established theories and implied that doctors were killing patients.'),
  ('A more mixed story','Historians note that Semmelweis was slow to publish his findings in full and could offer no theoretical explanation before germ theory, which contributed to their rejection; some of his students did publicise the results, so the \"lone genius\" narrative is simplified.'),
  ('Semmelweis reflex','The term \"Semmelweis reflex\" is used for the tendency to reject new evidence that contradicts established norms.')],
 ['wiki:Ignaz_Semmelweis'],'Ignaz_Semmelweis',end=1865),
X('martian-canals','The canals of Mars: a civilisation seen through a telescope',H,'debunked',2,1877,['telescopic observation','textual','spacecraft imaging'],
 "During the close approach of Mars in 1877 the Italian astronomer Giovanni Schiaparelli mapped the planet and named long straight features canali, meaning \"channels\" (the word had been used for Mars by Angelo Secchi in 1858). In English this was often read as \"canals\", implying construction. The American Percival Lowell founded an observatory in Flagstaff, Arizona, in 1894 and promoted the idea that Martians had built a vast irrigation network to carry water from the polar caps. Better telescopes in the early 20th century showed the canals were optical illusions, and spacecraft from Mariner 4 (1965) onward found no such features, with the possible exception of the canyon Valles Marineris.",
 [('Intelligent Martians','Lowell and his followers argued the regular network could only be artificial; the idea shaped science fiction, from H. G. Wells\'s The War of the Worlds onward.'),
  ('Optical illusion','Astronomers such as E. M. Antoniadi showed that the human eye tends to join faint, scattered markings into lines at the limit of visibility.'),
  ('Lasting pattern','The episode is sometimes compared with later claims of artificial structures on Mars, such as the \"Face on Mars\", as examples of the mind finding patterns in ambiguous images.')],
 ['wiki:Martian_canals'],'Martian_canals',end=1965),
X('n-rays','N-rays: a new radiation that existed only in the eye of the beholder',H,'debunked',2,1903,['laboratory experiment','textual (scientific papers)'],
 "In 1903 the respected French physicist Prosper-René Blondlot, at the University of Nancy, announced a new form of radiation, which he called N-rays, detected by slight brightening of a spark or a phosphorescent screen. Many other scientists reported confirming it and many papers were published, though leading physicists such as Lord Kelvin and William Crookes failed to replicate it. In 1904 the American physicist Robert W. Wood visited Blondlot's laboratory and, in the dark, secretly removed an essential prism from the apparatus; the experimenters still reported seeing the effect. Wood's account in Nature effectively ended belief in N-rays.",
 [('Experimenter bias','N-rays are a classic case of observers seeing what they expect, and are cited by Irving Langmuir as an example of \"pathological science\".'),
  ('Why many confirmed it','Writers on the episode point to the subjective nature of judging faint brightness changes by eye, the prestige of Blondlot and the excitement after the discovery of X-rays (1895) as reasons why many researchers reported seeing the effect.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/070530a0','wiki:N-ray'],'N-ray',end=1904),
X('krakatoa-1883','Krakatoa 1883: the loudest sound in history and a blood-red sky',H,'confirmed',1,1883,['eyewitness testimony','instrumental records (barographs, tide gauges)','geological'],
 "On 26–27 August 1883 the volcanic island of Krakatoa in the Sunda Strait between Java and Sumatra (in present-day Indonesia) erupted catastrophically. A series of huge explosions on 27 August, heard as far as Rodrigues near Mauritius about 4,800 km away, is considered the loudest sound in recorded history; barometers around the world recorded the pressure wave circling the globe more than three times. Tsunamis devastated nearby coasts, and at least 36,417 deaths are attributed to the eruption. Volcanic aerosols produced vivid red sunsets worldwide for months and cooled global temperatures. A new island, Anak Krakatau (\"Child of Krakatoa\"), emerged from the sea in 1927.",
 [('The Scream','Some researchers have argued that the red sky in Edvard Munch\'s The Scream (1893) reflects his memory of Krakatoa sunsets in Norway; others suggest nacreous (mother-of-pearl) clouds or simply artistic expression.'),
  ('Global event','The eruption was among the first natural disasters reported worldwide almost immediately via the telegraph network.'),
  ('Recorded skies','The British artist William Ascroft made thousands of colour sketches of the strange sunsets seen in London after the eruption, now a record of its atmospheric effects.')],
 ['wiki:1883_eruption_of_Krakatoa'],'1883_eruption_of_Krakatoa',dateNote='26–27 August 1883'),
X('devils-footprints','The Devil\'s Footprints: hoof-marks across the snow of Devon',H,'disputed',2,1855,['textual (newspapers, letters)','eyewitness testimony'],
 "On the night of 8–9 February 1855, after heavy snow, trails of hoof-like marks appeared around the Exe Estuary in Devon, England, covering an estimated 40 to 100 miles in total. The prints, about 4 inches long and mostly in a single line, were reported to pass over roofs and walls and through obstacles, and some locals attributed them to the Devil. The event was reported in the Illustrated London News and other newspapers.",
 [('Animal tracks','Proposed explanations include wood mice or other small animals hopping in the snow, badgers, donkeys and ponies, and birds; Mike Dash concluded there was no single source and that some trails were probably hoaxes.'),
  ('A runaway balloon','Author Geoffrey Household suggested an experimental balloon released from Devonport Dockyard trailing shackles on its mooring ropes; critics doubt it could have followed such a course without snagging.'),
  ('Exaggeration','Sceptics such as Joe Nickell argue that no one followed the whole trail, and that separate tracks were combined into one story by excited reports.')],
 ['wiki:Devil\'s_Footprints'],'Devil\'s_Footprints',dateNote='8–9 February 1855'),
X('halley-1910','Halley\'s Comet 1910: comet pills and the poison-tail panic',H,'confirmed',2,1910,['textual (newspapers)','spectroscopy'],
 "In February 1910 astronomers at Yerkes Observatory reported that the spectrum of Halley's Comet showed cyanogen, a poisonous gas, and it was known that Earth would pass through the comet's tail in May. The French astronomer and popular writer Camille Flammarion was reported as saying the gas could \"impregnate the atmosphere and possibly snuff out all life on the planet\". Although most astronomers explained that the tail was far too thin to cause harm, people in several countries bought \"anti-comet pills\", gas masks and comet umbrellas, sealed their homes, and held comet parties. Earth passed through the tail around 18–19 May 1910 without any effect. Mark Twain, born in the comet year of 1835, had predicted he would \"go out with it\"; he died on 21 April 1910, the day after the comet's perihelion.",
 [('Science misread','Historians use the episode as a case study in how a real scientific finding, amplified by newspapers, can turn into public panic.'),
  ('Ancient fear','Comets have been seen as omens of disaster in many cultures, including Halley\'s appearance before the Norman Conquest of 1066, so the 1910 panic drew on a very old tradition.'),
  ('Coincidence','Twain\'s death is often told as an uncanny fulfilment; sceptics note that he was 74 and in poor health.')],
 ['wiki:Halley\'s_Comet','url:https://www.sciencehistory.org/stories/magazine/the-comet-panic-of-1910-revisited/|Science History Institute: The Comet Panic of 1910, Revisited|magazine','url:https://guides.loc.gov/chronicling-america-halleys-comet|Library of Congress: Halley\'s Comet – Topics in Chronicling America|library guide'],'Halley\'s_Comet',dateNote='February–May 1910'),
X('kaspar-hauser','Kaspar Hauser: the boy from the dark cell, and the lost-prince theory',H,'confirmed',2,1828,['textual','forensic','DNA'],
 "On 26 May 1828 a teenage boy was found in Nuremberg, Bavaria, carrying two letters and barely able to speak. As Kaspar Hauser he said he had spent his whole childhood alone in a dark cell, fed bread and water by an unseen man. He became a European sensation, was wounded in mysterious attacks, and on 14 December 1833 came home in Ansbach with a deep stab wound, saying a stranger had attacked him in the Court Garden; he died on 17 December. A rumour spread that he was the hereditary prince of Baden, born in 1812 and supposedly switched at birth. DNA tests reported in 1996 and a 2024 study of several samples attributed to him found that his mitochondrial DNA did not match the House of Baden line.",
 [('Lost prince','For nearly two centuries supporters argued he was the Baden heir hidden for dynastic reasons and murdered to keep the secret; the 2024 genetic study is widely seen as ruling this out.'),
  ('Impostor','Sceptics at the time and since have argued that he exaggerated or invented his story and inflicted his own wounds, possibly including the fatal one by accident; a 1928 medical study supported self-infliction, while a 2005 forensic analysis was inconclusive.'),
  ('Feral child','Others see him as a genuinely neglected or abused child whose true origins remain unknown; he is often discussed alongside other \"feral children\".')],
 ['wiki:Kaspar_Hauser'],'Kaspar_Hauser',end=1833),
]
